faq:
  - q: "How do I change careers without direct experience in the new field?"
    a: "Career changers face a specific problem: most job listings require direct experience in the role, which creates a chicken-and-egg problem for people transitioning in. The strategies that work to break through: (1) Lead with transferable skills, not your previous title. Identify the 3–5 skills most critical to your target role and build your resume, LinkedIn, and interview stories around them — even if acquired in a different context. (2) Bridge roles are your fastest path. Roles that sit at the intersection of your current background and your target function (e.g., moving from accounting to FP&A before moving to investment banking; or teaching to curriculum design before moving to product management at EdTech) let you build direct credentials without starting from zero. (3) Build proof-of-work. For technical transitions: personal projects, GitHub portfolios, or freelance work. For creative transitions: a portfolio. For consulting or analysis: published writing or case studies. (4) Networking displaces credentials. A direct referral from someone inside a target company bypasses the ATS filter that eliminates career changers before a human reads their resume."
  - q: "What are the most successful career change paths?"
    a: "Some career transitions have well-worn paths with clear preparation requirements; others require more creative bridging. High-success career change paths: (1) Military → Corporate — veterans successfully enter consulting, operations, project management, and leadership development programs. MBA bridge programs (many business schools have military fellowships) are a common accelerator. (2) Teaching → Customer Success, EdTech, Instructional Design, or Sales — the communication and curriculum skills translate with the right resume framing. (3) Finance → FinTech, Corporate Strategy, or Private Equity — within-finance transitions with the right credentials. (4) Engineering → Product Management — a common and well-served transition path; bootcamps, APM programs, and internal transfers are all viable routes. (5) Law → Consulting, Business, or Policy — JD-holders command analytical credibility; the transition typically requires an MBA or a direct skills bridge. (6) Military, consulting, or finance → Venture Capital or Startups — operator experience is increasingly valued at growth-stage companies."
  - q: "How long does a career change realistically take?"
    a: "A career change to an adjacent function (e.g., marketing to product, finance to consulting) typically takes 3–9 months with focused effort. A career change to a significantly different domain (e.g., military to finance, teaching to software engineering, law to data science) typically takes 1–3 years — including any retraining, bridge roles, or credential-building required. The timeline compresses significantly when: the career changer networks actively rather than relying on applications, a bridge role is used strategically rather than as a dead end, the resume is rewritten to lead with transferable skills rather than the old title, and interview preparation is specific to the new industry's interview format (case interviews for consulting, technical screens for engineering, product cases for PM roles). The timeline extends when: the career changer waits for the 'perfect' role rather than a bridge role, applies only to senior roles in the target field without relevant credentials, or treats the job search as a passive activity."
  - q: "What does a career coach for career changers actually do?"
    a: "Career coaching for career changers is most valuable in three areas: (1) Clarity — identifying the most viable and satisfying target function given your actual skills and interests, rather than an aspirational target that will require 5 years to reach. Many career changers spend months targeting roles that are too large a leap and miss the bridge role that could get them there in 3 months. (2) Positioning — a career coach helps you find the authentic story that connects your background to your target — not fabricating experience, but articulating the real transferable skills in language the target industry recognizes. (3) Preparation — interview preparation for a career changer requires different content than for a direct candidate. You need to preempt the 'you don't have direct experience' concern before it becomes a rejection. Coaches who have made transitions themselves or hired career changers understand how to do this."
  - q: "How do I answer 'why are you switching careers?' in interviews?"
    a: "The 'why are you switching careers?' question is the most high-stakes question for career changers — and the most frequently mishandled. Answers that fail: (1) Negative answers — 'I was bored,' 'my old industry is dying,' 'I need better pay.' These signal flight risk and lack of genuine conviction. (2) Generic answers — 'I want a new challenge,' 'I've always been interested in X.' These sound rehearsed and fail to convince. Answers that work: (1) A pull story — you are drawn to the target role for specific, substantive reasons that reflect genuine knowledge of the role and field: 'I've been building X on the side for 2 years, and I've realized the work I find most energizing is Y, which is what this role does full time.' (2) A narrative arc — your previous experience built skills that are directly relevant to this role and this moment, and the move is a logical extension of where you've been heading: 'My work in Z taught me A and B. I'm now pursuing this role because it applies those skills to a problem I care more about.' The answer must be forward-looking, specific, and grounded in genuine knowledge of what the target role actually involves."
  - q: "Should I go back to school to change careers?"
    a: "Going back to school to change careers is high-cost and frequently unnecessary — but occasionally the right call. When school makes sense: an MBA for transitioning into management consulting, investment banking, or senior tech PM roles (where the MBA is a credential unlock, not just education). A coding bootcamp for transitioning into software engineering (adds technical credibility and a portfolio). A law degree if the target role genuinely requires JD-level legal knowledge. An advanced degree in data science or ML if the target employer (research lab, quant fund) genuinely requires it. When school is a mistake: when a bridge role exists that builds the same credential at lower cost and faster timeline. When the target employer cares about skills, not degree names (most startups, many mid-market companies). When the program's placement outcomes don't justify the cost in your specific target function. The test: can you land an informational interview with 3 people doing your target job and ask them honestly whether your background + school is better than your background + 1 year of bridge role experience? Their answer tells you more than any career advisor."
