      <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow">The career change job search strategy that works</h2>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>Network first, apply second.</strong> Online applications for career changers have very low response rates because ATS systems filter for direct experience keywords you don't have. A direct referral from inside the target company — reached through LinkedIn outreach or a shared connection — bypasses the filter entirely. For career changers, networking is not optional; it is the primary sourcing strategy.</li>
        <li><strong>Target adjacent roles, not aspirational ones.</strong> Applying directly to senior roles in your target function when you have no direct experience is almost always unsuccessful. The bridge role strategy — taking a role that is 70% your current background and 30% your target — builds the credentials that make the full transition possible in 12–18 months rather than 3–5 years.</li>
        <li><strong>Build proof-of-work before the interview.</strong> Whatever you can do to demonstrate your capability in the new field — a personal project, a freelance engagement, a published piece of analysis, a completed certification — dramatically strengthens your candidacy compared to a theoretical claim of transferable skills.</li>
        <li><strong>Your story must make the move feel inevitable, not impulsive.</strong> Career changers who can articulate a coherent narrative — where they came from, what they built, what they realized, and why this move is the logical next step — are compelling. Career changers who seem to be escaping something are not.</li>
      </ul>
